Simulated ICS Network Threat Detection Lab

📌 Overview

This project simulates a realistic Industrial Control System (ICS) environment to analyze Modbus TCP traffic, identify legitimate vs. suspicious behavior, and mimic how security tools like firewalls and SIEMs handle OT network threats. Designed and implemented over 2–3 days, the lab offers a hands-on dive into packet-level threat detection for SCADA systems.

🎯 Objectives

Simulate ICS/SCADA devices using Conpot
Generate legitimate and malicious Modbus traffic
Use ACL-based IP filtering to mimic firewall behavior
Capture and analyze traffic with Wireshark
Build a Python-based detection script to flag suspicious activity
(Optional) Forward alerts to Splunk for visualization

⚙️ Tools & Technologies

Conpot (ICS honeypot)
Modpoll (Modbus traffic generator)
iptables (for ACL simulation)
Wireshark (packet capture & analysis)
Python (Scapy + PyShark for detection logic)
Splunk Cloud (optional SIEM integration)

🧪 Phase 1: ICS Simulation Setup

Set up Conpot container to simulate a Modbus server
Used modpoll to generate Modbus TCP requests (function code 3)
Created local IP aliasing (127.0.0.2) to simulate unauthorized devices
Verified traffic flows using Wireshark

Challenges:

Modpoll failed initially due to incorrect target port
Conpot default port was 5020, not 502 → resolved by redirecting traffic

📡 Phase 2: Traffic Capture & Labeling

Captured both clean and suspicious Modbus traffic
Legitimate requests originated from 127.0.0.1
Suspicious requests originated from 127.0.0.2, blocked via iptables and redirected to honeypot
Used Wireshark to analyze TCP flags and Modbus function codes

Insights:

Suspicious traffic often resulted in TCP RST
Clean traffic had full query-response cycles
🧠 Phase 3: Detection Logic (Python)
Built a script using Scapy and PyShark to:
Detect Modbus Function Codes (esp. code 3)
Flag traffic from unauthorized IPs
Detect TCP RSTs and connection attempts with no Modbus layer

Key Features:

Legitimate Modbus traffic flagged as [✅ OK]
Suspicious or blocked traffic flagged as [🚨 ALERT] or [⚠️ BLOCKED]

🔐 Phase 4: ACL Simulation

Used iptables to:
Allow traffic from 127.0.0.1
Redirect/block traffic from 127.0.0.2
Used socat to simulate connection attempts from spoofed IPs

Effect:

Created realistic ACL behavior to enforce Modbus access control

📈 Phase 5: (Optional) SIEM Integration

Created alert JSONs in Python
Attempted Splunk Cloud forwarding via HEC
Faced DNS resolution issues due to trial version limitations

Next Steps:

Deploy Splunk Enterprise locally for full integration
Extend to support Universal Forwarder or syslog

